---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Is Marine Engineering a Good Career? Pay, COC Exams, Verdict';
const description =
  'Is marine engineering a good career? Real pay by rank, the actual COC exam ladder from 4th Engineer to Chief Engineer, the placement-scam risk, and shore-based paths after sea time.';
const canonical = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/is-marine-engineering-a-good-career';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-08-17';
const publishedDateLabel = '17 August 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'admission-routes', label: 'How you actually get into marine engineering' },
  { id: 'daily-work', label: 'What a marine engineer actually does' },
  { id: 'pay-reality', label: 'Real pay by rank' },
  { id: 'rank-progression', label: 'The COC exam ladder: 4th to Chief Engineer' },
  { id: 'lifestyle-reality', label: 'The honest lifestyle trade-off' },
  { id: 'placement-risk', label: 'The placement-scam risk nobody warns you about' },
  { id: 'ai-risk', label: 'What engine-room automation actually changes' },
  { id: 'who-fits', label: 'Who this path genuinely fits' },
  { id: 'who-pause', label: 'Who should think twice' },
  { id: 'checkpoint', label: 'The 4-Checkpoint Protocol' },
  { id: 'gates', label: 'The 3 Gates before you commit' },
  { id: 'verdict', label: 'The verdict framework' },
  { id: 'alternatives', label: 'Shore-based paths after sea time' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'Mistakes to avoid' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const admissionRows = [
  {
    route: 'B.Tech / B.E. Marine Engineering via IMU-CET',
    detail:
      'The Indian Maritime University Common Entrance Test (IMU-CET) is the main gateway into B.Tech Marine Engineering at IMU\'s own campuses and its DG Shipping-approved affiliate colleges. A PCM background in 12th is required, and the exam is the single biggest filter most aspirants underestimate.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Diploma in Marine Engineering (DME / GME)',
    detail:
      'A shorter, more affordable route: roughly one year of pre-sea academic training after 12th (PCM), followed directly by sea time as a trainee. It reaches the same 4th Engineer starting point faster than a degree, but narrows some later shore-side options that specifically ask for a B.Tech.',
  },
  {
    route: 'DG Shipping approval, not the college brand, is what actually matters',
    detail:
      'Every legitimate marine engineering institute in India must be approved by the Directorate General of Shipping (DG Shipping) to issue valid pre-sea training certificates. An unapproved or newly-set-up institute with a glossy campus and no real DG Shipping approval number is a hard stop, whatever the brochure claims.',
  },
  {
    route: 'Pre-sea training and workshop simulator time',
    detail:
      'Before any sea time counts, cadets complete mandatory pre-sea training: engine-room simulators, workshop practice, safety and firefighting modules, and STCW basic training. This is where a genuinely good institute separates itself from a placement mill, because simulator and workshop quality directly shapes how fast a cadet becomes ship-ready.',
  },
];

const payRows = [
  {
    rank: 'Trainee Marine Engineer / Junior Engineer',
    pay: 'Rs 15,000-35,000 per month during pre-sea training and the first sea contract',
    reality: 'This stage is about accumulating mandatory sea time and passing the MEO Class IV written and oral exams, not income. Pay varies widely by the shipping company sponsoring the cadetship.',
  },
  {
    rank: '4th Engineer (first watchkeeping officer rank)',
    pay: 'Rs 3.5-6 LPA equivalent, paid as a monthly USD salary during active contracts',
    reality: 'Reached only after clearing the MEO Class IV Certificate of Competency exam. This is the first rank where the officer independently keeps an engine-room watch.',
  },
  {
    rank: '3rd Engineer',
    pay: 'Rs 6-10 LPA equivalent',
    reality: 'Usually an internal promotion from 4th Engineer on the same MEO Class IV certificate, awarded once a company sees enough demonstrated watchkeeping competence and sea time, not a separate exam stage.',
  },
  {
    rank: '2nd Engineer',
    pay: 'Rs 12-22 LPA equivalent',
    reality: 'Requires passing the MEO Class II Certificate of Competency exam after the mandated sea time as a watchkeeping officer. The 2nd Engineer runs the daily engine-room operation and reports directly to the Chief Engineer.',
  },
  {
    rank: 'Chief Engineer',
    pay: 'Rs 25-45 LPA equivalent and above, often more on LNG carriers or chemical tankers',
    reality: 'Requires passing the MEO Class I Certificate of Competency exam, the toughest and most technically demanding of the four. Chief Engineer carries full legal and technical responsibility for the ship\'s entire machinery plant.',
  },
];

const rankProgressionItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Junior Engineer to 4th Engineer: the MEO Class IV certificate',
    body: 'After pre-sea training, a cadet sails as a Trainee Marine Engineer, logging roughly 12 months of supervised sea time. To be promoted to 4th Engineer, the candidate must pass the MEO Class IV written exams (covering marine engineering knowledge, naval architecture basics, and applied mechanics) plus an oral exam with a DG Shipping-approved examiner.',
    note: 'This is the certificate that legally permits someone to keep an independent engine-room watch for the first time. Without it, no shipping company can sign you on as an officer, no matter how much sea time you have logged.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: '4th Engineer to 3rd Engineer: sea time, not a new exam',
    body: 'Most officers move from 4th to 3rd Engineer under the same MEO Class IV certificate, typically after 6-12 months of demonstrated watchkeeping reliability. This step depends more on company appraisal and consistent performance reports than on any additional written test.',
    note: 'Officers who delay building a track record of clean watch handovers and machinery logs at this stage often find promotion slower than peers who treat every contract as a proof-of-work opportunity.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: '3rd Engineer to 2nd Engineer: the MEO Class II certificate',
    body: 'After accumulating the required sea time as a watchkeeping officer (commonly totalling 24-30 months across 4th and 3rd Engineer ranks), the officer sits the MEO Class II exams, a substantially harder written and oral test covering machinery operation, maintenance planning, and engine-room management.',
    note: 'This is genuinely the steepest technical jump in the whole ladder. Officers who start structured revision during their leave period between contracts, rather than cramming just before the exam window, pass on the first attempt far more often.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: '2nd Engineer to Chief Engineer: the MEO Class I certificate',
    body: 'After further sea time as 2nd Engineer, usually another 24-36 months depending on vessel type and company policy, the officer sits the MEO Class I exams, the top-level Certificate of Competency covering full technical, legal, and managerial responsibility for the ship\'s machinery.',
    note: 'From cadetship to Chief Engineer typically runs 8-12 years of accumulated sea time and exam stages, not a fixed number, and depends heavily on how consistently the officer sails, revises, and clears each COC exam on the first attempt rather than repeating it.',
  },
];

const lifestyleComparison = {
  gold: [
    'Sea contracts on the engine side typically run 5-8 months continuously aboard the vessel, with almost no ability to step ashore during that window beyond brief port calls, and often limited or no internet access below deck.',
    'Officer income during active contracts is genuinely tax-advantaged under Indian tax rules for qualifying seafaring income, which meaningfully raises real take-home pay compared with a similarly experienced shore-based mechanical or marine engineer.',
    'Leave between contracts, often 2-4 months, is substantial and largely unstructured — some officers use it to revise for the next COC exam or build a side skill, others find the repeated cycle of long separation followed by long idle time personally disorienting over several years.',
  ],
  teal: [
    'The isolation is compounded for engine officers specifically: watchkeeping shifts (commonly a 4-on, 8-off rotation) mean broken sleep, heat, noise, and machinery-space confinement for most of each working day, not just time away from family.',
    'Family separation, not pay dissatisfaction, is consistently the reason officers cite for eventually moving ashore, and this is worth an honest conversation with a partner or family before committing years of training to the pipeline.',
    'Many experienced engine officers eventually move into shore-based technical roles once they have banked enough sea time and COC progress, using their qualification as a credential rather than sailing for an entire career.',
  ],
};

const scamRows = [
  {
    pattern: 'Manning agents demanding a large upfront "registration" or "processing" fee for a guaranteed berth',
    reality: 'Legitimate manning agents are paid by the shipping company, not by the candidate, for placing a qualified officer. A large upfront fee tied to a "guaranteed job" promise is one of the most common scam patterns reported in Indian seafarer forums and DG Shipping advisories.',
  },
  {
    pattern: 'Fake or inflated sea-time entries and doctored Continuous Discharge Certificates (CDC)',
    reality: 'Some intermediaries offer to "arrange" extra sea time on paper without genuine onboard service. This is not a shortcut; it is a fraud that can permanently disqualify a candidate\'s COC application once discovered during verification.',
  },
  {
    pattern: 'Training institutes claiming guaranteed placement without a real, verifiable ship-owner tie-up',
    reality: 'Placement quality and genuine shipping-company relationships vary enormously between institutes despite near-identical marketing language. A brochure claim of "100% placement" means nothing without a specific, checkable list of companies that actually hired last year\'s batch.',
  },
  {
    pattern: 'Recruiters operating without a valid, current RPSL number',
    reality: 'Every legitimate recruitment and placement agent in Indian shipping must hold a Recruitment and Placement Service Licence (RPSL) issued by DG Shipping. Checking an agent\'s RPSL number directly against DG Shipping\'s published list, before sharing documents or paying any fee, is the single most effective scam filter available.',
  },
];

const checkpointItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Biology',
    body: 'Can you genuinely sustain 4-on, 8-off watchkeeping rotations in a hot, loud engine room for 5-8 month stretches, with broken sleep and limited personal space, without it wearing you down?',
    note: 'The engine department\'s physical demands are different from the deck side: heat, noise, and machinery-space confinement are a daily reality, not an occasional inconvenience.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Context',
    body: 'Can your current or near-future family situation genuinely absorb months of continuous separation, repeated every contract cycle for a decade or more while you climb the COC ladder?',
    note: 'This decision affects a partner, children, or ageing parents as much as it affects you. It deserves an honest conversation before, not after, you commit to the training pipeline.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Market',
    body: 'Are you targeting a DG Shipping-approved institute with a verifiable placement track record and a real plan to clear each MEO exam promptly, or a generic entry with no plan beyond the first contract?',
    note: 'Officers who plan deliberately toward MEO Class II and Class I, rather than drifting at junior rank, build genuinely stronger careers and reach higher pay meaningfully faster.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Survival',
    body: 'Given real engine-room automation, condition-based monitoring, and predictive-maintenance sensors, is your plan to build genuine hands-on troubleshooting and machinery judgment, or to rely only on routine watchkeeping tasks that software increasingly assists?',
    note: 'The officers who stay valuable are the ones who can read the sensor data and still physically diagnose and fix a failing pump at 3 a.m. mid-ocean, not the ones who only monitor a screen.',
  },
];

const gateItems = [
  {
    num: 'Gate 1',
    title: 'Proof of skill',
    body: 'Sit through a genuine engine-room simulator session or workshop demonstration at a shortlisted DG Shipping-approved institute, and ask to see actual machinery, not just a promotional video, before enrolling.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 2',
    title: 'Proof of communication',
    body: 'Explain, in under two minutes, to your family exactly what a 5-8 month contract with limited connectivity genuinely means for time together, repeated for a decade of rank progression. If this conversation has not happened yet, have it before you commit.',
  },
  {
    num: 'Gate 3',
    title: 'Proof of value',
    body: 'Talk directly to a working 2nd Engineer or Chief Engineer, not just an admissions counsellor, and ask honestly about their COC exam experience, their real sea-time timeline, and whether they would choose this path again.',
  },
];

const verdictYes = [
  'You are genuinely comfortable with, and have discussed with family, the reality of repeated 5-8 month sea contracts with engine-room watchkeeping shifts.',
  'You have a real interest in mechanical, thermal, and electrical systems specifically, not just the general idea of a seagoing career.',
  'You are prioritising strong, tax-advantaged early-career income and are willing to trade lifestyle flexibility and years of exam preparation for it.',
  'You have a realistic plan to clear MEO Class IV, II, and I on schedule, plus, eventually, a deliberate plan for a shore-based technical role rather than an open-ended assumption about sailing indefinitely.',
];

const verdictNo = [
  'You are choosing marine engineering mainly for the pay without seriously weighing the watchkeeping-shift and extended-separation lifestyle.',
  'You are in, or planning, a relationship or family situation that genuinely needs consistent physical presence, and have not had an honest conversation about sea contracts.',
  'You are relying on a training institute\'s "guaranteed placement" claim, or a manning agent asking for a large upfront fee, without independently verifying either.',
  'You dislike the idea of three separate, genuinely difficult Certificate of Competency exams as a structural, recurring part of career progression.',
];

const alternativeCards = [
  {
    title: 'Ship management and technical superintendency, once you have banked meaningful sea time',
    body: 'Ship management companies hire experienced marine engineers ashore as technical superintendents, overseeing maintenance, dry-docking, and compliance for a fleet of vessels from a shore office. Sea experience is treated as a genuine, valued credential, not a formality, for these roles.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Marine surveying, if you enjoy inspection, documentation, and technical judgment more than hands-on repair',
    body: 'Marine surveyors inspect vessel machinery, hull condition, and cargo systems on behalf of owners, charterers, or insurers, and typically require several years of sea service before a surveying qualification is even open to you.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Classification societies, if long-term technical assessment work appeals more than a purely commercial shipping career',
    body: 'Organisations such as RINA, ABS (American Bureau of Shipping), DNV, and the Indian Register of Shipping (IRClass) employ marine engineers as surveyors and technical assessors who certify that vessels meet international safety and construction standards, a genuinely different, shore-based technical career built directly on sea-service credibility.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Marine insurance and P&I club technical claims work, if the appeal is the engineering knowledge without the sailing',
    body: 'Protection and Indemnity (P&I) clubs and marine insurers hire former marine engineers to assess machinery-failure claims and technical disputes, work that draws directly on hands-on engine-room experience from a desk, not a ship.',
  },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Choosing an institute without checking its DG Shipping approval and real placement record',
    body: 'A DG Shipping approval number and a verifiable list of companies that actually hired last year\'s graduating batch matter far more than campus photos or a placement percentage claimed in a brochure.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Paying a large upfront fee to a manning agent for a "guaranteed" berth',
    body: 'Legitimate agents are paid by shipping companies for placing qualified officers, not by candidates for a promised job. Always verify the agent\'s current RPSL number against DG Shipping\'s published list before sharing documents or money.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Delaying MEO exam attempts instead of preparing during leave periods',
    body: 'Promotion through officer ranks is gated by passing MEO Class IV, II, and I exams at each stage. Officers who use leave time between contracts to revise systematically clear these exams meaningfully faster than officers who wait and cram.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Not having an honest conversation with family before committing to the training pipeline',
    body: 'Repeated 5-8 month sea contracts, spread across a decade of rank progression, affect relationships in ways that are hard to fully appreciate before living through the first contract. This conversation belongs before enrolment, not after the first COC exam.',
  },
  {
    num: '05',
    title: 'Having no plan for a shore-based transition once sea time and COC progress are banked',
    body: 'Ship management, marine surveying, and classification-society roles all value real sea experience as a credential. Planning this transition deliberately, rather than treating it as an afterthought once sea life becomes unsustainable, leads to a stronger, more strategic career.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'Is marine engineering a good career in India in 2026?',
    a: 'Yes, for people genuinely comfortable with watchkeeping-shift life at sea and three demanding Certificate of Competency exams on the way to Chief Engineer. Pay ranges from roughly Rs 3.5-6 LPA equivalent at 4th Engineer to Rs 25-45 LPA equivalent and above at Chief Engineer, largely tax-advantaged for genuine seafaring income. The honest trade-off is real: 5-8 month sea contracts with limited connectivity and physically demanding engine-room shifts are the defining feature, not the pay.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What is the salary of a marine engineer in India by rank?',
    a: 'Trainee and Junior Engineers earn a modest Rs 15,000-35,000 per month during pre-sea training and early contracts. 4th Engineers, the first watchkeeping officer rank after clearing MEO Class IV, earn roughly Rs 3.5-6 LPA equivalent, rising to Rs 6-10 LPA at 3rd Engineer, Rs 12-22 LPA at 2nd Engineer after clearing MEO Class II, and Rs 25-45 LPA equivalent and above at Chief Engineer after clearing MEO Class I, often more on LNG carriers or chemical tankers.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What exams do you need to become a Chief Engineer?',
    a: 'Three Certificate of Competency (COC) exams gate the entire ladder: MEO Class IV to become a 4th Engineer and keep an independent watch, MEO Class II to become a 2nd Engineer after the required sea time as a watchkeeping officer, and MEO Class I, the toughest of the three, to become Chief Engineer. Promotion from 4th to 3rd Engineer usually happens on the same MEO Class IV certificate through sea time and company appraisal, without a separate exam.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How do I verify a marine engineering institute or manning agent is legitimate?',
    a: 'Confirm the institute holds current DG Shipping approval, and ask for a specific, checkable list of companies that hired its most recent graduating batch, not a generic placement percentage. For manning agents, verify their Recruitment and Placement Service Licence (RPSL) number directly against DG Shipping\'s published list before sharing documents or paying any fee. A large upfront "registration" fee tied to a guaranteed berth is a common scam pattern worth treating as a hard warning sign.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What can a marine engineer do after leaving sea life?',
    a: 'Sea experience translates directly into several genuine shore-based technical careers: ship management and technical superintendency overseeing fleet maintenance, marine surveying inspecting vessel machinery and hull condition, classification-society work at organisations like RINA, ABS, DNV, or IRClass certifying vessels against safety standards, and marine insurance or P&I club technical claims work. All of these value real sea-service credentials over a fresh shore-only hire.',
  },
];

const faqJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
    '@type': 'Question',
    name: item.q,
    acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
  })),
};

const relatedGuides = [
  { href: '/blog/career-options/is-merchant-navy-a-good-career', label: 'Is merchant navy a good career? Deck vs engine, real pay, verdict' },
  { href: '/blog/career-options/is-mechanical-engineering-a-good-career-in-india', label: 'Is mechanical engineering a good career in India? Pay, EV boom, real catch' },
  { href: '/blog/career-options/best-career-options-with-high-salary', label: 'Best career options with high salary in India' },
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={[faqJsonLd]}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    <strong>Is marine engineering a good career? Yes, genuinely, for people honestly prepared for its real
    trade-off: strong, tax-advantaged pay and a defined Certificate of Competency exam ladder, in exchange for
    5-8 month watchkeeping-shift contracts at sea, away from family.</strong>
    Pay runs from roughly Rs 3.5-6 LPA equivalent at 4th Engineer to Rs 25-45 LPA equivalent and above at Chief
    Engineer, but the officer rank alone does not decide the outcome — clearing MEO Class IV, II, and I on
    schedule does. Approached with a real plan and a genuine <strong>high-value skill portfolio</strong> built
    around hands-on machinery judgment, it remains a real route to <strong>earlier financial freedom</strong>
    and strong <strong>high income opportunities</strong> relatively early in a career.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Marine engineering is the engine department of a ship, entered through B.Tech Marine Engineering (via IMU-CET) or a shorter Diploma in Marine Engineering, both requiring DG Shipping-approved institutes.</li>
      <li>Rank progression runs on three real exams: MEO Class IV (4th Engineer), MEO Class II (2nd Engineer), and MEO Class I (Chief Engineer), spread across roughly 8-12 years of sea time and revision.</li>
      <li>Pay runs from Rs 3.5-6 LPA equivalent at 4th Engineer to Rs 25-45 LPA equivalent and above at Chief Engineer, typically tax-advantaged for genuine seafaring income.</li>
      <li><strong>Placement fraud is a real, documented risk in this field</strong> — verify a manning agent's RPSL number and an institute's DG Shipping approval directly before paying any fee or signing anything.</li>
      <li>Sea contracts of 5-8 months with watchkeeping shifts, not pay, are the defining lifestyle trade-off and the top reason officers eventually move ashore.</li>
      <li>Ship management, marine surveying, and classification societies (RINA, ABS, DNV, IRClass) are genuine shore-based paths once sea time and COC progress are banked.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <p>
    If you are weighing this against the broader deck-versus-engine merchant navy decision, reading
    <a href="/blog/career-options/is-merchant-navy-a-good-career">is merchant navy a good career</a> first can help
    you confirm whether hands-on mechanical and electrical systems genuinely pull you more than navigation and
    ship command does, before you narrow down to marine engineering specifically.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you want a second opinion on whether the exam-heavy, sea-time-heavy structure of this path genuinely fits
    your situation, a session inside
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career guidance</a>
    is worth having before you commit years of training and a real education budget to it.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Article sections">
    <p class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</p>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((item) => (
        <li><a href={`#${item.id}`}>{item.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is marine engineering a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    Marine engineering remains a genuinely strong, technical career in India for the right person, but the
    honest answer depends on two things most recruitment marketing glosses over: whether you can sustain
    repeated watchkeeping-shift sea contracts, and whether you have a real plan to clear three demanding
    Certificate of Competency exams on schedule.
  </p>

  <p>
    The pay is genuinely strong and largely tax-advantaged. The exam ladder is genuinely difficult and gates
    every promotion. Both are true at once, and the career rewards officers who plan deliberately around both,
    not officers who assume sea time alone will carry them to Chief Engineer.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      This is not the "see the world and earn tax-free income" pitch that some maritime academy marketing leans
      on, and it is not a warning to avoid the engine department either. Both miss the real picture.
    </p>
    <p>
      The pay genuinely is strong once you reach 2nd Engineer and above. The MEO exam ladder genuinely is hard,
      and the watchkeeping-shift lifestyle genuinely is difficult for many officers over a decade of sailing.
      The honest decision depends on whether you are prepared for the specific combination of mechanical
      problem-solving, recurring high-stakes exams, and extended sea time this career actually demands.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="admission-routes">How you actually get into marine engineering</h2>

  <p>
    Before pay or lifestyle, the entry route deserves its own honest look, because this is where a lot of
    families lose money to weak institutes before a single day at sea.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Route</th>
          <th>What actually matters</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {admissionRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Route"><strong>{item.route}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="What actually matters">{item.detail}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--amber">
    <p>
      Before enrolling anywhere, confirm the institute's current DG Shipping approval number directly and ask
      for a specific, checkable list of shipping companies that hired last year's graduating batch. A glossy
      campus and a vague "100% placement" claim are not proof of either.
    </p>
    <p>
      Fees at private DG Shipping-approved marine engineering colleges can run high, often Rs 8-18 lakh across
      a full B.Tech programme once hostel and simulator charges are added. Treat roughly 10% of your family's
      total education budget as a strict starting benchmark for the college itself, and only spend materially
      more when the specific institute has a verified placement record and real simulator or workshop quality
      to show for it, not just a bigger campus.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="daily-work">What a marine engineer actually does</h2>

  <p>
    The engine department keeps a ship's propulsion, power generation, fuel systems, and auxiliary machinery
    running around the clock. Watchkeeping officers work rotating shifts, commonly a 4-on, 8-off cycle, monitoring
    engine parameters, running maintenance checks, and responding to machinery alarms in a hot, loud, confined
    engine room.
  </p>

  <p>
    The boring 80% of the job is routine log-keeping, planned maintenance, spare-parts tracking, and machinery
    checks. The satisfying part, actually diagnosing and fixing a failing system mid-voyage with no shore-based
    backup, is real, but it is a smaller share of most weeks than recruitment marketing suggests.
  </p>

  <h2 id="pay-reality">Real pay by rank</h2>

  <p>
    A single "marine engineer salary" figure hides more than it reveals, because a trainee and a Chief Engineer
    earn wildly different amounts, gated by specific exams rather than years alone.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Rank</th>
          <th>Typical range</th>
          <th>Reality</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {payRows.map((item) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Rank"><strong>{item.rank}</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Typical range">{item.pay}</td>
            <td data-label="Reality">{item.reality}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p class="note-line">
    Ranges are directional, based on aggregated 2025-2026 industry data at the time of writing, and often paid in
    USD per contract. Verify current figures against live shipping-company listings for the specific vessel type
    and rank before making a financial decision.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rank-progression">The COC exam ladder: 4th to Chief Engineer</h2>

  <p>
    This is the part most generic merchant navy overviews skip past with a single line about "STCW competency
    exams." The engine department's promotion path runs on three specific, named certificates, each genuinely
    harder than the last.
  </p>

  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {rankProgressionItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="checkpoint">
        <div class="checkpoint__num">{item.num}</div>
