  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Before paying any agent, sharing your Continuous Discharge Certificate, or signing a contract, verify the
      agent's Recruitment and Placement Service Licence (RPSL) number directly on DG Shipping's published list.
      This single check filters out most of the fraud in this field, and it costs nothing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="ai-risk">What engine-room automation actually changes, honestly</h2>

  <p>
    Most "is marine engineering a good career" searches skip this question, but rising engine-room automation
    headlines make it worth a direct, honest answer.
  </p>

  <p>
    Condition-based monitoring, predictive-maintenance sensors, and unmanned machinery space (UMS) systems are
    real and increasingly standard on modern vessels. These tools genuinely reduce routine manual monitoring and
    flag developing faults earlier than a human alone would catch them.
  </p>

  <p>
    What they do not do, at least not on a near-term horizon, is replace the hands-on judgment needed to
    physically diagnose and repair a failing pump, generator, or fuel system mid-voyage with no shore-based
    backup available. A more honest read: engine officers who build genuine comfort reading sensor and
    diagnostic data, as a normal part of the job rather than something to resist, are better positioned as
    these systems mature further over the coming decade. Officers whose entire value is routine watchkeeping
    with no troubleshooting depth are competing in a shrinking task category.
  </p>

  <h2 id="who-fits">Who this path genuinely fits</h2>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You have real comfort with mechanical, thermal, and electrical systems, not just an interest in ships generally</div>
      <p>The engine room rewards people who like physically reasoning through how machinery runs, heats, and fails, and who do not mind long stretches of troubleshooting before a system actually works right.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You can sustain watchkeeping-shift life and repeated 5-8 month sea contracts without significant distress</div>
      <p>Broken sleep, heat, and confinement in a machinery space are a daily reality on this path, not an occasional inconvenience. Being honest about your own tolerance for this matters more than enthusiasm for engineering alone.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="feature-card">
      <div class="feature-card__label">Genuine fit</div>
      <div class="feature-card__title">You are genuinely willing to prepare for three separate, high-stakes exams across a decade</div>
      <p>MEO Class IV, II, and I each gate real career progression and real pay jumps. Officers who treat leave time as revision time consistently outpace officers who hope sea time alone will be enough.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h2 id="who-pause">Who should think twice before choosing marine engineering</h2>

  <p>
    This is the section most maritime academy marketing skips, because it does not make for good admissions
    copy. It is the section that saves a genuinely difficult few years discovering the reality the hard way.
  </p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="paths-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Warning sign</th>
          <th>What is actually true</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>You are choosing this mainly for the pay without weighing the watchkeeping-shift lifestyle</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Extended separation and disrupted sleep, not pay, are consistently the reasons engine officers eventually move ashore. Weigh both sides honestly before committing to the training pipeline.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>You are relying on a training institute's "guaranteed placement" claim at face value</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Placement quality and ship-owner tie-ups vary significantly between institutes despite near-identical marketing. Verify a shortlisted institute's real, recent placement outcomes directly, not brochure claims.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>You dislike the idea of three separate, recurring high-stakes exams</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Promotion through every officer rank is gated by MEO Class IV, II, and I. This is a career with genuine, structured, high-stakes testing built into its progression, not a one-time qualification.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td data-label="Warning sign"><strong>You are in, or planning, a relationship that needs consistent physical presence</strong></td>
          <td data-label="What is actually true">Sea contracts of 5-8 months, repeated across a decade of rank progression, genuinely strain relationships and parenting in ways worth discussing openly with a partner before committing.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

